# =============================================================================
# Local SpamAssassin overrides
#
# Loaded from /etc/spamassassin/ (site config dir) after the stock rules in
# /usr/share/spamassassin/. Ships via `COPY rootfs/ /` in the Dockerfile, so a
# change here needs an image rebuild + container recreate to take effect.
#
# NOTE: /etc/mail/spamassassin is a symlink to /etc/spamassassin on Debian /
# Ubuntu, so this single file covers both paths.
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Validity (ex-Return Path / Habeas) DNSBLs — DISABLED (2026-08-07)
#
# All three zones are decommissioned and now answer EVERY query with:
#   A   127.255.255.255
#   TXT "Excessive Number of Queries | https://knowledge.validity.com/..."
#
# The stock rules use check_rbl_txt() / check_rbl() with no return-code filter,
# so all three matched on every single sender IP (verified: even 127.0.0.1 and
# 8.8.8.8 "hit" the certification whitelist). Standing effect on every inbound
# message:
#
#   RCVD_IN_VALIDITY_CERTIFIED  -3.000   (bogus whitelist credit)
#   RCVD_IN_VALIDITY_SAFE       -2.000   (bogus whitelist credit)
#   RCVD_IN_VALIDITY_RPBL       +1.284   (bogus blocklist penalty)
#   ------------------------------------
#   net                         -3.716
#
# With $sa_tag2_level_deflt = 5.0 that put a -3.72 floor under every message and
# effectively disabled spam tagging (723 "Passed CLEAN" vs 2 "Passed SPAM" in
# the log before this fix). Setting a score of 0 both zeroes the rule and makes
# SpamAssassin skip the (useless) DNS lookup entirely.
#
# Upstream already fixed this: the current sa-update ruleset adds a return-code
# filter ('^127\.0\.0\.') to the rules, adds dedicated *_BLOCKED rules matching
# 127.255.255.255, and scores all of them 0. init.sh now runs sa-update at
# container start (plus scripts/sa-update.sh weekly via cron), so in the normal
# case the updated ruleset already handles it.
#
# This file is therefore a deliberate safety net, not the primary fix: it keeps
# the Validity rules neutral even if sa-update fails, is blocked by a firewall,
# or the ./data/spamassassin bind mount is ever wiped. Scoring a rule 0 also
# makes SpamAssassin skip the (useless) DNS lookup entirely.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
score RCVD_IN_VALIDITY_CERTIFIED 0
score RCVD_IN_VALIDITY_SAFE 0
score RCVD_IN_VALIDITY_RPBL 0
